    {
      "id": "770d2d02-4576-4653-9164-4eca5f3ec488",
      "type": "playlists",
      "attributes": {
        "name": "Label Focus: Creation Records",
        "description": "Founded in 1983 by Alan McGee, Dick Green and Joe Foster, Creation Records remains one of the most culturally significant independent labels in recent memory. Throughout the 1980s and '90s, the British outfit headed by indie impresario – and notorious wild man – Alan McGee put out a number of tremendously influential releases, shaping such genres as acid house, shoegaze, post-rock, and Britpop in the process. But while iconic records like My Bloody Valentine’s ‘Loveless,’ Teenage Fanclub’s ‘Bandwagonesque’ and Primal Scream’s ‘Screamadelica’ – all released in 1991 – would garner immense critical acclaim, Creation would first strike mainstream commercial gold with Oasis’ 1994 debut ‘Definitely Maybe’, which they quickly followed up in 1995 with their monumental sophomore LP ‘(What’s The Story) Morning Glory?’. Taking the scrappy little label to unprecedented commercial heights, the record would become the best selling British album of the decade, with over 22 million units sold to date. Burned out and disillusioned after 16 strenuous years of non-stop partying, hedonism, madness and music, McGee and Foster agreed to shut down the label in 1999, with Primal Scream’s ‘XTRMNTR’ marking Creation Record’s final release. In an effort to tell this legendary label’s sonic story, we’ve compiled a playlist of its highlights featuring the likes of Oasis, My Bloody Valentine, Ride, Slowdive, Primal Scream, Teenage Fanclub, The Jesus and Mary Chain and many more.\n\n(Credit: Creation Records, press)",
        "bounded": true,
        "duration": "PT3H9M20S",
