    {
      "id": "c85bbce9-223a-4ffb-8b4d-9cd7028e1f44",
      "type": "playlists",
      "attributes": {
        "name": "Made in Germany: Krautrock",
        "description": "In the late 1960s a new sound emerged in Germany, sparked by a generation growing up in the dark shadows of the war. Leaning on the counter culture movement, but performing music less dependent on Anglo-American styles, this movement was closer associated with the likes of avant-garde, jazz, minimalism, Eastern influences and electronics. A wave of pioneering groups emerged at the end of the decade, epitomized by bands like Can, Kluster, Tangerine Dream, Faust, Neu! and Kraftwerk. British media coined them all as “Krautrock”, but their characteristics were more diverse, their sound more nuanced, than could aptly fit in one bag – from the ambient soundscapes of Tangerine Dream to the rhythmic outbursts of Can, from the motorik beats of Neu! to the global playground of Embryo. Even so, we’ve gathered just a few examples of Krautrock here, largely picked from the “golden era” in the early to mid 1970s. Most of these tracks belong more naturally in the context of an album, so we encourage you to use this list as a starting point for deeper explorations. With the exception of Kraftwerk, Krautrock did not gain any huge commercial success, but the influence of these bands can be traced from David Bowie to Stereolab to Wilco, from spacerock to shoegaze, post-punk, new wave and beyond. Kosmiche music, indeed. (Photo: Unsplash)",
        "bounded": true,
        "duration": "PT3H46M24S",
        "numberOfItems": 28,
        "externalLinks": [
