    {
      "id": "cf42bb98-2734-4cf5-9326-cce048b13388",
      "type": "playlists",
      "attributes": {
        "name": "Pillows of Noise: Shoegaze Classics",
        "description": "Shoegaze emerged in 1980s Britain; a loosely based term coined after a number of bands appeared with introspective looks and a motionless style, more looking down at their guitar pedals than facing the audience. My Bloody Valentine is probably the quintessential and most influential shoegaze band, but related acts like The Jesus & Mary Chain, Ride and Slowdive are all are considered among the most important names from a scene also jokingly referred to as “the scene that celebrates itself.\" Distinct features include loud walls of driving guitars, the prominent use of effect pedals, soft waves of noise and wrenched, dreamlike vocals. Shoegaze is closely connected to other sub-genres of indie rock, like dream pop, post-rock and later nu-gaze. The scene's various players are as plentiful as its highlights, and, without drawing any firm lines, we give you some prime examples of this beloved style. Crank it up and close your eyes. (Photo: Unsplash)",
        "bounded": true,
        "duration": "PT4H5M16S",
        "numberOfItems": 50,
        "externalLinks": [
